<?xml version="1.0"?>
<system name="failures"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="http://jsbsim.sourceforge.net/JSBSimSystem.xsd">

<!-- ===================================================================
     Control-surface failure chain (ADVANCEMENTS_BLUEPRINT section 1,
     correction 1; work item P2).

     One chain per surface: the command the host (or the TECS system,
     which runs before this one) writes to fcs/<surface>-cmd-norm is
     scaled by failure/<surface>/authority, passed through a JSBSim
     <actuator> that carries JSBSim's own malfunction switches
     (failure/<surface>/actuator/malfunction/fail_stuck, fail_zero,
     fail_hardover; FGActuator.cpp), clipped to -1..1 and written to
     failure/<surface>/cmd-in. The airframe's <flight_control> input for
     the surface is RE-ANCHORED by core/control/derive.py to read
     failure/<surface>/cmd-in instead of fcs/<surface>-cmd-norm.

     WHY THE CHAIN DOES NOT WRITE fcs/<surface>-cmd-norm BACK
     =====================================================
     A pass-through actuator reading and writing the same property
     applies the authority gain CUMULATIVELY: authority 0.5 with a
     command written once decays -0.15, -0.075, -0.0375 ... to zero in
     about ten steps (measured; the skeptic's correction 1). The host
     writes control inputs only at scheduled times, so any authority
     below 1 would float to zero. Reading the host's property and
     writing a NEW one leaves the command where the host put it.

     NEUTRAL VALUES
     ==============
     authority 1.0 and no malfunction: the chain is x * 1.0, clipped to
     the range the airframe's own trim summer clips to anyway, so the
     derived airframe is bit-identical to the stock one over a trimmed
     elevator-step run (measured in tests/test_derive_injections.py).

     NOT CLAIMED: no rate limit, lag or hysteresis (the stock airframes
     declare none and this chain adds none); no hydraulic topology; a
     command beyond -1..1 is clipped here before the airframe's trim
     summer sees it (the host never writes one).

     The template is expanded by core/control/derive.py: @SURFACE@ is
     stamped once per surface in the fixed order elevator, aileron,
     rudder; the derivation refuses derivation.anchor_missing when the
     airframe's <flight_control> does not read fcs/<surface>-cmd-norm
     through an <input> for every one of them.
     =================================================================== -->

<!--PROPERTIES-->

<!--CHANNELS-->

</system>
<!--SURFACE_PROPERTIES
  <property value="1.0">failure/@SURFACE@/authority</property>
  <property value="0.0">failure/@SURFACE@/cmd-in</property>
SURFACE_PROPERTIES-->
